(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cphims=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims.full||[]).push({
  num: 3, part: `Part One: The Inheritance`, title: `The Plumbing`, weeks: `Months 2 to 3`,
  tasks: [`E7`,`E6`,`SB1`,`SB2`,`SC6`,`C2`,`L22`],
  cast: [
    [`Tasha Greene`,`Service desk manager, Cascade Valley Health. Lives in ticket data. Sees every problem a week before anyone else.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Relay lived in the basement of the Fort Collins data center, in a room that had been a storage closet until 2010. Marcus Bell had moved into it then and never left. Three monitors, a space heater he wasn't supposed to have, a coffee maker he definitely wasn't supposed to have, and a whiteboard covered in boxes and arrows that nobody else in the building could read.`,
    `Maria came down at 8:15 on Tuesday, straight from her one-on-one with Ochoa. He'd read her status page and asked one question. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Quiet, pointed, unhurried.">"Do you know why yet?"</span> She'd said Marcus did. Ochoa had said, <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Then go find out what he knows. All of it. Write it down this time."</span>`,
    `Marcus didn't look up when she knocked. He was a big man in a fleece vest, gray at the temples, twenty-two years into a career of making systems talk to each other that had never been built to. He'd written most of Relay himself, one interface at a time, and he kept the rest of it in his head.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Sit,"</span> he said, and pointed at a folding chair. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"This takes a minute. It's two problems. The first one is the boring one, and it's the one everybody's going to want to talk about."</span>`,
    `On the middle monitor was a diagram she half recognized. A box for Relay. A line to Laramie. A smaller box on the line marked Cheyenne firewall, and someone had circled it in red, twice.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Plumbing,"</span> Marcus said. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Everybody thinks the EHR is the hospital. It's not. It's a bunch of systems that don't know each other, and Relay is the pipes in the walls. You don't think about pipes until the ceiling's wet."</span>`,
    `Maria pulled the chair up. For the next two months, she'd think about almost nothing else.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ochoa's Legal Pad: pipes, words, and the people who know`,
    sections: [
      { h: `The pipes in the walls`,
        body: [`Thursday, Ochoa came down to the basement himself. Marcus said that had never happened, not once in sixteen years. He stood at the whiteboard with a marker he'd borrowed and didn't give back. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Every system you own sits on top of this,"</span> he said, and drew three layers.`,
          `The bottom layer is the network. The local area network connects devices inside a building. The wide area network connects the buildings. It's how Laramie reaches Fort Collins. A virtual private network, or VPN, builds a secure tunnel across a network you don't control. A firewall decides what traffic gets to cross between networks. It keeps track of open connections, too. That's how it can close one. Wireless carries the phones, the computers on wheels, and more of the medical devices every year. Underneath it all sit quiet services nobody thinks about until they break, like the time server every device uses to set its clock.`,
          `The middle layer is integration. Point to point interfaces connect each pair of systems directly, which works for five systems and turns into a hairball at fifty. An interface engine, like Relay, sits in the middle as a hub: each system sends to the engine once, and the engine translates, routes, queues, and logs. HL7 version 2 messages usually travel over a long lived connection using a simple wrapper called MLLP. The receiver answers each message with an acknowledgment, or ACK. An application accept means the receiver processed it. An error or reject means it didn't, and the engine has to decide what to do next. Some systems first send a commit accept, which only means the message was received and safely stored.`,
          `The top layer is the applications Maria owns. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Clinicians only ever see the top,"</span> Ochoa said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"So when the middle fails, they think the top is slow."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Interface engine`,`Middleware that receives, translates, routes, queues, and logs messages between systems, so each system connects once to a hub instead of to every other system.`],[`ACK`,`An HL7 acknowledgment. The receiver tells the sender whether a message was accepted, had an error, or was rejected.`],[`Firewall`,`A device or service that controls which network traffic may pass between networks, and tracks open connections.`]],
        exam: `Expect questions on hub and spoke versus point to point. An interface engine reduces the number of connections, centralizes monitoring, and becomes a critical component that needs redundancy and its own support plan.` },
      { h: `The messages`,
        body: [`Ochoa wrote the standards down the side of the board, one family per line. HL7 version 2 is the workhorse inside hospitals: pipe delimited messages made of segments. An ADT message tells everyone where a patient is. A01 is an admission, A02 a transfer, A03 a discharge, A04 an outpatient registration, A08 an update to patient information. An ORM or OML message carries an order, and an ORU message carries a result. Inside, MSH is the header, PID identifies the patient, PV1 describes the visit, OBR describes the order, and OBX carries each result value.`,
          `CDA, the Clinical Document Architecture, packages a whole clinical document in XML. Its consolidated form, C-CDA, includes the Continuity of Care Document, the summary that follows a patient at a transfer or referral. FHIR, Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources, breaks data into small resources, like Patient, Observation, and MedicationRequest, exchanged over modern web APIs. It's what patient apps and newer integrations use.`,
          `Outside the clinical record there are other families. DICOM moves and stores medical images, along with the information about each image. X12 carries business transactions with payers: the 837 claim, the 835 payment and remittance, the 270 and 271 eligibility question and answer. NCPDP SCRIPT carries electronic prescriptions between prescribers and pharmacies, and NCPDP has a separate standard for pharmacy claims.`],
        terms: [[`HL7 v2`,`The most widely used messaging standard inside hospitals, built from segments such as MSH, PID, PV1, OBR, and OBX.`],[`C-CDA`,`Consolidated Clinical Document Architecture. Templates for clinical documents, including the Continuity of Care Document used at transitions.`],[`FHIR`,`Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources. An HL7 standard that exchanges data as small resources over web APIs.`]],
        exam: `Match the standard to the job. Images are DICOM. Claims and eligibility are X12. E-prescribing is NCPDP SCRIPT. Hospital messages are usually HL7 v2. Documents at transitions are C-CDA. App and API access points to FHIR.` },
      { h: `The words inside the messages`,
        body: [`A message that arrives perfectly can still mean nothing if the two systems use different words. That's why vocabularies exist. SNOMED CT names clinical findings, problems, and procedures in detail. LOINC names lab tests and clinical observations, so a lactate is the same test in Laramie and Fort Collins. RxNorm normalizes drug names and strengths. ICD-10-CM codes diagnoses, and ICD-10-PCS codes inpatient hospital procedures. CPT, owned by the American Medical Association, codes the services physicians and outpatient settings bill for.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Interoperability has levels,"</span> Ochoa said. HIMSS describes four. Foundational: the data can get from one system to another. Structural: the format is agreed, so the receiver can parse it. Semantic: both sides mean the same thing by the codes, so the data can be used. Organizational: the governance, policy, consent, and trust that let organizations actually share it. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Most of your trouble lives at semantic and organizational. The wire is the easy part."</span>`],
        terms: [[`LOINC`,`Logical Observation Identifiers Names and Codes. The standard vocabulary for lab tests and clinical observations.`],[`Semantic interoperability`,`Systems share data and agree on what it means, usually through standard vocabularies, so it can be used without translation by a person.`],[`RxNorm`,`A normalized naming system for clinical drugs, maintained by the National Library of Medicine.`]],
        exam: `Know which vocabulary covers what. Lab tests are LOINC, clinical terms are SNOMED CT, drugs are RxNorm, diagnoses are ICD-10-CM, and physician services are CPT. When two systems exchange data but can't use it, the gap is semantic.` },
      { h: `Devices are on the network too`,
        body: [`Bedside monitors, ventilators, and infusion pumps send data into the record through a device integration gateway. Smart pumps can receive an order from the EHR so the nurse doesn't program the rate by hand, then send back what the pump actually did. Every one of those devices has a clock, a network address, and software that needs patches. Clinical engineering, sometimes called biomed, owns the devices. IT owns the network they sit on. Both have to agree before anything changes, and many organizations put medical devices on their own segment of the network to protect them. Profiles from IHE, Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise, describe how standards fit together for jobs like this.`],
        terms: [[`Medical device integration`,`Moving data between bedside devices and the EHR through a gateway, so values are captured automatically and accurately.`],[`Clinical engineering`,`The department, often called biomed, that manages, maintains, and tests medical equipment.`]],
        exam: `When a device sends wrong or mistimed data, the right answer usually brings clinical engineering, IT, and the vendor together, fixes the source, and validates before clinicians rely on it again.` },
      { h: `Watch the plumbing`,
        body: [`You can't fix what you don't see. Network monitoring watches links, devices, and bandwidth. Interface monitoring watches the things clinicians feel: queue depth, the age of the oldest message, error and reject rates, and whether ACKs are coming back. Help desk logs, error reports, and user surveys show the same problems from the other side, often earlier. A trend matters more than one event. Alerts are only as good as the person who receives them, at the hour they fire, with a written step to take.`,
          `Ochoa capped the marker and looked at Marcus, then at Maria. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"And people are infrastructure. If one person is the only one who can read this board, that's a single point of failure, same as one firewall. You fix it the same way. Redundancy, and practice."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Queue depth`,`The number of messages waiting in an interface engine to be delivered.`],[`Single point of failure`,`Any one component, or person, whose failure stops the whole system.`]],
        exam: `Monitoring questions reward the answer that watches what matters to patient care, routes alerts to someone on duty around the clock, and tests that the alert actually reaches them.` }
    ],
    video: [`wEhUgy5wQ1Q`,`HL7 Explained for Beginners | Healthcare Interoperability: The Big Picture | Lesson 1`,`RCM Pulse Academy`,`12:32`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f3s1`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E7`, title:`Too patient`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Marcus walked her through it the way he'd walk a new nurse through a drip. Relay held one open connection to MedaLink at Laramie, across the Wyoming network, through a firewall in Cheyenne. That connection had stayed open, day and night, for nine years.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Two Thursdays ago the change board approved a firewall cleanup. Listed as no clinical impact. One line in it cut the idle timeout from eight hours to sixty minutes. Laramie's lab goes quiet some nights between seven and eight. At 8:28 the firewall decided my connection was dead and dropped it. Didn't tell anybody. Relay kept sending into it, waiting for an ACK that was never coming. And I'd set it to wait forever, because in 2017 forever seemed safe."</span>`,
        (G.solofix
          ?`He tapped the log. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus" data-tone="Even, plain, not unkind.">"Also. The restart at 11:56 came from your account. It cleared the connection history I needed. Took me four extra days to rebuild it from the firewall side."</span> He didn't say it unkindly. He didn't have to.`
          :`He tapped the log. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"The emergency change note had my timestamps, so the firewall team found their side in ten minutes. That part went right."</span>`),
        `So what does Maria want fixed?`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask the network team to set the firewall timeout back to eight hours tonight.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The network team does it the same day. Laramie is safe again. Six weeks later a different firewall at Rawlins gets a different cleanup, and nobody remembers that Relay cares.`,
          why:`You fixed the change that broke it. You didn't fix why one setting on one firewall could silently stop lab results. The real gaps are an engine that waits forever and a change process that doesn't know what depends on the network.`},
        {t:`Freeze firewall changes on the Wyoming network until One Chart is live.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Nate's team pushes back within the hour. Two security patches for those firewalls are already overdue. A freeze trades a known interface risk for a known security one.`,
          why:`Freezing infrastructure feels safe and isn't. Networks need patches and changes. What you need is for changes to know what they touch, and for the systems on top to recover by themselves.`},
        {t:`Have Relay time out and reconnect, and list its links for the change board.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Marcus sets an ACK timeout of thirty seconds, an automatic reconnect, and a heartbeat message every five minutes. The network team adds every Relay connection to the list the change board checks before approving any firewall work. Marcus tests it by having the network team drop the Laramie session on purpose. Relay reconnects in forty seconds and files a note saying it did.`,
          why:`Fix both ends. The application layer should detect a dead connection and recover on its own, and the change process should know which clinical systems ride on each piece of the network. Then test the fix by breaking it on purpose.`},
        {t:`Have Marcus check each Wyoming connection by hand every evening at shift change.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Marcus does it for eleven days. On the twelfth he's at his granddaughter's recital, and nobody checks.`,
          why:`A manual check by one person isn't a control. It's a hope with a calendar reminder. Automate the detection and the recovery, and keep people for the judgment calls.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s2`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SC6`, title:`The folder`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Now the one you came for."</span> Marcus opened his email. Relay had sent an alert at 8:41 PM, thirteen minutes after the connection died. Queue depth over threshold, Laramie lab results. It had gone to exactly one person.`,`It sat in a folder called Relay noise. The folder held 31,412 unread messages. Marcus scrolled for a long time to show her. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Thresholds were set in 2015, when we had half the traffic. It fires forty times a day for nothing. So I filtered it. Everybody filters it. That's the part I wanted you sitting down for."</span>`,`That afternoon Tasha Greene, the service desk manager, sent two more things without being asked. Two tickets from Laramie nurses, 9:10 PM and 10:02 PM. Labs slow? Logged as application slowness, low priority, closed the next morning as resolved.`,
        `<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="Relay queue depth, Laramie lab results, the night of the outage" labels="8 PM,9 PM,10 PM,11 PM,11:56 PM,12:30 AM" values="0,384,1104,1824,2496,0">Messages waiting in the queue. Nothing moved from 8:28 PM until the restart at 11:56. The queue was empty by 12:30.</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Lower the alert threshold and add the whole interface team to the email list.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Now four people get forty alerts a day for nothing, and one real one. Within a month all four have filters.`,
          why:`More people on an alert nobody trusts just spreads the filter. The problem is signal and routing: the wrong measures, the wrong thresholds, and no one on duty at 8:41 PM whose job it is to act.`},
        {t:`Send tuned interface alerts to the 24/7 desk with a runbook, then test them.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`Marcus and Tasha rebuild the alerts around what nurses feel: the age of the oldest message and whether ACKs are coming back. Alerts page the service desk, which works around the clock, and the desk has a one-page runbook and an on-call engineer to call. Tasha links any ticket that says labs slow to interface status. They test it at 2 PM on a Wednesday by pausing a test feed. The desk calls Marcus in six minutes.`,
          why:`Analyze the whole signal chain: what's measured, the threshold, who receives it, at what hour, and what they're supposed to do. Help desk tickets are monitoring data too. And an alert you haven't tested is a guess.`},
        {t:`Buy a modern monitoring platform that can watch every interface out of the box.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The vendor demo is lovely. The quote takes a month, the build takes six, and the new tool would have emailed the same alert to the same inbox.`,
          why:`A new tool doesn't fix bad thresholds or bad routing. Figure out what failed in the process first. Often the tool you have is fine once someone tunes it and owns it.`},
        {t:`Ask Marcus to clear the folder and read every Relay alert as it comes in.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Marcus looks at her for a long moment. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus" data-tone="Flat, deadpan, sarcastic.">"Sure. Every night. Forever."</span> He doesn't say anything else that day.`,
          why:`You found the gap and handed it back to the one person who couldn't close it. A system that depends on one man reading forty false alarms a day has already failed. Fix the system.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s3`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SC6`, title:`The readout`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The patient safety review met Friday in a Laramie conference room with a speakerphone in the middle of the table. Fourteen people, among them the patient safety officer, the lab, the house supervisor, two nurse managers, a network engineer, Marcus, and Maria.`,`Maria walked through the night. The firewall change. The connection that waited forever. The alert at 8:41. The folder.`,`The Laramie quality director looked up from her notes. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"So the alert worked. It went to one person, and he didn't read it."</span>`,`Every head in the room turned to Marcus. He looked at the table.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Say yes, the alert reached Marcus, and she'll handle that with him privately.`, s:0, flag:`blamedmarcus`, d:{trust:-6,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`It's technically true. The room relaxes the way rooms do once they have a name. Marcus doesn't speak for the rest of the meeting, and he leaves before the action items.`,
          why:`You just closed the review with the least useful answer it could reach. A person who missed one alert among thirty thousand isn't a root cause. The design that sent it there is. And you said it in front of his peers.`},
        {t:`Say it's a personnel question, and steer the meeting back to the firewall change.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The room moves on. But nobody corrected the quality director, and three people leave believing it was Marcus.`,
          why:`You protected him from a public blame, which is decent. You also left the wrong conclusion standing. Say what the analysis shows, out loud, while the room is listening.`},
        {t:`Say the alert went where the design sent it, and list every contributing cause.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Level, plain, owning it.">"The alert went where we built it to go. One inbox, no on-call path, thresholds from 2015. That's mine."</span> Then she lists the rest: a firewall change approved without a dependency check, an engine that waited forever, two nurse tickets triaged as slowness. The patient safety officer writes all four on the board. Marcus looks up for the first time.`,
          why:`Root cause analysis looks for the conditions that let a failure happen, and there's almost never just one. Name the system causes, own the ones that are yours, and keep the review from settling on the nearest person. That's how people keep reporting problems to you.`},
        {t:`Ask the network engineer to explain the firewall change, since it started it all.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The network engineer turns red and explains the change board approved it. Now the room has two people to blame instead of one.`,
          why:`Moving the blame from one person to another isn't analysis. The firewall change was one cause among several. Present the chain, not a culprit.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s4`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E7`, title:`Her own key`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The next Wednesday Nate Ostrowski, the CISO, sent Maria a list. His team had pulled every account with administrator rights on Relay. There were nine.`,`Marcus. Two consultants who'd left in 2019 and 2022. Four service accounts nobody could name an owner for. A shared account called relayadmin whose password, Nate wrote, was apparently known to four people and taped inside one drawer. And Maria Santos, granted for the Penwright interfaces during the build, never removed.`,
        (G.solofix
          ?`Nate had added one line under hers. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"Used once in the last ninety days. 11:56 PM, the night of the Laramie outage. Restart of a production interface, no change ticket."</span>`
          :`Nate had added one line under hers. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"Not used in ninety days. That's good. It's also why it shouldn't exist."</span>`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask Nate to remove her access today, and clean up all nine with Marcus.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She writes back in two lines: remove mine today, and let's do the rest together. The consultants' accounts go first. The shared account takes three weeks, because Marcus's scripts use it, and each script gets its own service account with only the rights it needs. Nate replies with one word. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"Appreciated."</span>`,
          why:`Least privilege applies to the people at the top, too. Access should match the job you do now, not a job you used to have. Lead by removing your own, then fix the rest: named accounts, no shared logins, and service accounts scoped to what they do.`},
        {t:`Keep her access as a backup, in case Marcus can't be reached during an outage.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Nate writes back one line. <span class="said" data-who="Nate" data-tone="Cool, flat, unimpressed.">"A backup who isn't on call, isn't trained, and doesn't open change tickets isn't a backup."</span>`,
          why:`The answer to one engineer being a single point of failure is more trained engineers on call, not a vice president with admin rights. Access you don't need is risk you're carrying for no reason.`},
        {t:`Change hers to read-only, so she can still watch the queues during an outage.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`It's better. She can see the queues without being able to change them. But she hasn't looked once in three months, and the new alerts already go to people who will.`,
          why:`Read-only is a real improvement over admin. Still ask whether you need it at all. If the monitoring works, a leader gets the dashboard, not a login to production.`},
        {t:`Remove the consultants now, and leave the shared account since scripts need it.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Two dead accounts go away. The account four people share, with a password in a drawer, stays. It's the one an attacker would want.`,
          why:`A shared admin account means no one can tell who did what. If scripts need access, give each one its own service account with only the rights it uses. Convenience isn't a reason to keep a shared key.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s5`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E6`, title:`Which ones first`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The new alerts took real work for each feed, and Marcus could do about twenty a month. Relay carried 214 interfaces.`,`Kiran printed the list and spread it across Maria's table. It read like a map of the whole health system. Registration's ADT feed out to the lab, pharmacy, radiology, dietary, and billing. Orders out, results back. Pharmacy to the dispensing cabinets. Images from PACS. Charges to the billing system. Census and ADT to Lumen, the discharge forecasting tool. The HIE. The portal. Two state reports. A feed to a dietary system that, as far as anyone could tell, had been turned off in 2020 and was still sending.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Twenty a month means eleven months,"</span> Kiran said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Where do you want to start?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Start with the busiest feeds, since the most messages means the most exposure.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The busiest feed is a supply chain update that runs every minute. It gets month one. A results feed with a hundred messages a day, all of them critical values for an ICU, waits until August.`,
          why:`Volume measures traffic, not harm. Rank by what happens to a patient if the feed stops: results, orders, ADT, and medication feeds first, then financial and reporting.`},
        {t:`Rank by patient harm if each feed stops, with each application owner signing off.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`They score every interface on one question: if this stops for three hours, what happens to a patient? Results, orders, ADT, and pharmacy land on top. Lumen and the billing feeds land in the middle, which revenue cycle grumbles about and accepts. Each application owner signs their row. The dietary feed gets turned off for real.`,
          why:`Know your applications by family and by what they do for care. Clinical feeds that carry results, orders, location, and medications come first, then administrative and financial, then business intelligence. The owners of each application confirm the ranking.`},
        {t:`Start with the feeds that drew the most help desk tickets over the past year.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:2},
          after:`Tasha's data is good, and it catches two noisy feeds. It misses the quiet ones. The Laramie lab feed had two tickets all year.`,
          why:`Ticket history is a smart input, and Tasha's data will matter more and more. But the worst failures are often silent. Combine trend data with clinical risk.`},
        {t:`Let each application owner request slots, first come, first served, through intake.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Revenue cycle puts in a request within the hour. Radiology, the next morning. The lab director is on vacation, so the lab results feeds don't make the first three months.`,
          why:`Intake is good governance for new work, but order of arrival isn't priority. You own the interfaces. Set the ranking by risk, and let the owners confirm it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s6`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C2`, title:`The closed visits`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The Laramie backlog had filed, nearly 2,500 messages of it. Thirty-seven had come back from MedaLink with an error ACK. Marcus forwarded the reason, along with a message he'd translated into plain words for her.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="One ADT message, in plain words">Message type A03, a discharge. Sent by registration at Laramie, 9:47 PM. Patient identification: medical record number 4 4 7 1 9 0 2. Visit: account number 8 8 1 3 0 4 5 5, medical surgical unit, room 214. Discharge date and time: 9:45 PM. Disposition: home.</prop>`,
        `Patients had been discharged while their results sat in the queue. When those results finally arrived, MedaLink refused to file them on visits that were already closed. Thirty-seven lab results, some normal, a few not, belonged to patients who were already home.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have Marcus change the account numbers so they file to each patient's newest visit.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`They file. Three of them land on visits that started a week later, where they look like new results. A clinic nurse calls a patient about a potassium from a hospital stay that ended days ago.`,
          why:`The account number ties a result to the encounter where it was ordered. Changing it makes the record say something false. A result belongs to the visit it came from, and the clinician who ordered it needs to know it arrived.`},
        {t:`Reopen all thirty-seven visits in bulk, file everything, and let HIM sort it out.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The results file on the right visits, which is better. But nobody tells the physicians who discharged those patients, and two abnormal values sit on closed charts that nobody's looking at.`,
          why:`Filing to the right encounter is correct. A result pending at discharge is a known safety gap, though. Filing it isn't the end. Someone has to read it.`},
        {t:`Match the results to whichever chart is open, using each patient's name and birth date.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-5},
          after:`Laramie has two patients named Miller with the same birth date. One result files to the wrong one.`,
          why:`Name and birth date aren't unique. The medical record number and the master patient index identify the person, and the account number identifies the visit. Never match clinical data on demographics alone.`},
        {t:`Work all thirty-seven with HIM and the lab on the right visits, and tell each physician.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:6},
          after:`HIM confirms each patient against the master patient index and files each result to its own account number. The lab flags the six abnormal values. Kiran sends each discharging physician a list of their patients' late results. One gets a phone call the same night about a hemoglobin of 6.9.`,
          why:`Know the words. The medical record number identifies the patient, the account or visit number identifies the encounter, and the master patient index keeps one person from becoming two. File each result where it belongs, then close the loop with the clinician.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s7`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SB2`, title:`Three names for lactate`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Aisha Rahman caught Maria after the Clinical Informatics Council the second week of December. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"I want lactate times across all eleven hospitals. Order to result. I'm building something for sepsis and I need a baseline."</span>`,`The eight Corvane hospitals sent lactate with a LOINC code. The three MedaLink hospitals sent it as LACT, LA2, or LACTWB, depending on the hospital and the year someone built it. Laramie had nine hundred local lab codes in all.`,`Kiran was already nodding. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quick, eager, too cheerful.">"I can map all nine hundred. Weekends, maybe a month. It's fine."</span> He said it the way he said yes to everything, before anyone had finished asking.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Let Kiran map all nine hundred himself, since he knows lab codes better than anyone.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:-1},
          after:`He does it in five weekends. The mapping is excellent and nobody else has checked it. Two codes for a whole blood test and a plasma test end up on the same LOINC.`,
          why:`Mapping to a standard is the right move. A single analyst mapping clinical codes alone, unchecked, is a quality risk and a person risk. The lab owns what its tests mean.`},
        {t:`Have the lab own a LOINC map, with Kiran building it and the lab validating.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The Laramie lab director assigns a medical technologist to own the map. Kiran builds it in Relay, a hundred codes a week, and the lab validates each batch against real specimens. Lactate is done first. Aisha gets her baseline in January, and every code the lab signs is one less to fix at One Chart.`,
          why:`Use the standard vocabulary, and let the owner of the meaning decide it. LOINC is the standard for lab tests. The lab owns what each test is, IT builds the translation, and validation proves it's right before anyone uses the data.`},
        {t:`Build a translation table inside Aisha's report that just handles the lactate codes.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`It works for lactate. In February Aisha wants procalcitonin, and someone builds another table in another report. By spring there are four.`,
          why:`A custom translation hidden in one report solves one question once. Map to the standard at the interface, where every downstream system benefits.`},
        {t:`Wait for One Chart, since Corvane will use LOINC codes at all three hospitals.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Aisha's baseline waits most of a year. And when One Chart's build starts, the mapping still has to happen, just under a deadline.`,
          why:`The migration will need the mapping anyway. Doing it now gives clinicians usable data today and takes work off the go-live critical path.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s8`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SB1`, title:`Seven minutes`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The Laramie ICU nurse manager called Maria directly. She'd been reviewing a rapid response from the week before, and the timeline didn't make sense.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"The monitor says his pressure dropped at 3:14. My nurse called the team at 3:09. She didn't call them before it happened. The vitals in MedaLink are seven minutes off, and I think they've been off for a while."</span>`,`Marcus found it by the end of the day. The bedside monitors sent vitals through a device gateway, and the gateway stamped them with its own clock. The gateway set its clock from a time server that had been retired in 2023. It had been drifting ever since.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have Relay add seven minutes to every vitals message on its way into MedaLink.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`It works for a week. The drift keeps growing, and now the correction is wrong in a new way that nobody's watching.`,
          why:`Patching bad data in the interface hides the cause and drifts out of date. Fix the source. Interoperability includes the hardware and network services under the data.`},
        {t:`Ask the monitor vendor to come out, inspect the gateway, and correct its clock.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The vendor's field engineer comes in nine days, resets the clock, and leaves. The gateway is still pointed at a time server that doesn't exist, so it starts drifting again.`,
          why:`The vendor belongs in it. But the gateway sits on your network, using your time service, and biomed owns the device. A one-time reset by one party misses the cause.`},
        {t:`Have nurses chart vitals by hand at Laramie until One Chart replaces the gateway.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The nurses add twenty minutes of charting a shift. Manual vitals have their own errors, and the monitors keep sending wrong times anyway.`,
          why:`Turning off integration to avoid a fix shifts the burden onto the people you're supposed to help. Fix the device integration and validate it.`},
        {t:`Get biomed and network to fix every device's time source, then check against an event.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`Clinical engineering and the network team point the gateway, the monitors, and the pumps at Cascade's time service. They check other Wyoming device gateways and find one more. Then they trigger a test alarm at a set minute and confirm it files at that minute in MedaLink. The nurse manager gets a written note that her nurse called on time.`,
          why:`Medical device interoperability depends on hardware, software, and network services working together. Clinical engineering owns the devices, IT owns the network and time service, and the fix is proven by validation, not assumed.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s9`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E7`, title:`Page two`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The call came from a pharmacist at Cheyenne, three days before Christmas. A patient transferred from Rawlins had arrived with twenty medications on the transfer summary. His wife had brought a paper list from home. It had twenty-three.`,`Marcus found it in an hour. MedaLink's continuity of care document export had a limit of twenty rows in the medication section, set in a 2012 build. Anything past twenty was dropped. The document didn't say it was cut short. It just ended.`,`Maria read the email twice. Then she sat back in her chair and looked at nothing for a while.`,`She'd held a medication list with a page missing once. Greeley, her first year, a transfer from a small hospital in Wyoming. She didn't tell Marcus the rest. She didn't really tell herself.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fix the export template in the next build cycle, and tell Cheyenne pharmacy to watch for it.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The fix is scheduled for late January. Cheyenne pharmacy watches. The other two hospitals that receive from Rawlins don't know to.`,
          why:`Fixing the template is right. A known defect that drops medications at a transition is a patient safety issue now, not next sprint. It needs a report, a workaround, and a look back.`},
        {t:`Report it, add a manual check on big med lists until it's fixed, and look back.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:7},
          after:`She files a safety event that afternoon. Until the fix, nurses sending a transfer from any MedaLink hospital call the receiving pharmacist when a patient has more than twenty medications. Kiran pulls every transfer from the last two years: forty-one patients had more than twenty. Pharmacy reviews each one. Marcus has the template fixed in eight days, with a line that says when a list is cut off, just in case.`,
          why:`Data integration failures at transitions are patient safety events. Report it, protect patients now with a workaround, fix the source, and look back for anyone it already reached. And make the document say when it's incomplete.`},
        {t:`Add it to the One Chart requirements, since MedaLink retires in under two years.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Until go-live, every Wyoming transfer with more than twenty medications arrives short. Nobody knows which ones.`,
          why:`A replacement project isn't a fix for a live safety defect. If patients are at risk now, fix it now.`},
        {t:`Ask the regional HIE to flag any medication list that arrives with exactly twenty items.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The HIE says, politely, that it passes documents through and doesn't edit them. And transfers within Cascade don't go through the HIE at all.`,
          why:`The defect is in the sending system you own. Asking a downstream party to guess at a symptom isn't a fix, and it doesn't cover the paths that skip them.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s10`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E6`, title:`Production`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The Laramie quality director wanted one more number for the safety review: lab turnaround time, order to result, for the whole month around the outage, by hour. The data warehouse team said it would be easy if they could connect straight to MedaLink's production database.`,`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Read only,"</span> the warehouse lead said. <span class="said" data-who="man">"After hours. We'll be careful."</span>`,`Marcus, copied on the email, replied with a single line. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Ask them what happened in March 2024."</span> In March 2024 a report had run against MedaLink production at 2 PM and the nurses at Cheyenne couldn't chart for forty minutes.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Grant read-only production access, but only after midnight and only for this report.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The query runs at 1 AM and takes four hours, because MedaLink's database wasn't built for it. Night shift at Rawlins charts slowly until dawn and doesn't know why.`,
          why:`Production clinical systems are built to process transactions, not to answer big analytic questions. Nights are still patient care. Keep reporting off production.`},
        {t:`Have Marcus pull turnaround times out of Relay's own message logs for them.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Relay's logs show when results passed through the engine. They don't show when the specimen was collected or when a nurse saw the result, which is most of turnaround time.`,
          why:`Interface logs are useful for the interface part. Turnaround time spans several applications. Use the reporting system that brings them together.`},
        {t:`Use the warehouse's nightly copy of MedaLink and keep analytics off production.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`The warehouse already loads MedaLink every night. The order and result times are there. The team builds the report from the copy in three days. Marcus sends Maria a thumbs up, which from Marcus is a speech.`,
          why:`Business intelligence runs on a warehouse or reporting copy, built for analysis. Transaction systems serve patient care. Separating them protects performance and gives analysts data shaped for questions.`},
        {t:`Ask MedaLink's vendor to run the turnaround report for Cascade, as a support request.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`The vendor quotes six weeks and a fee. The safety review would be closed by then.`,
          why:`You already own the data in a form built for reporting. Paying a vendor that's ending support to reach into production for you is the slow road to the same risk.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s11`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E6`, title:`Year end`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Relay needed a patch, the first in two years. The change board approved a two hour window for the night of December 29. Every queue would hold during the window and drain after.`,`The Wyoming revenue cycle director called Maria the day before. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"It's year end. Every lab and pharmacy charge has to drop before the thirty-first, or it's next year's money and a mess for the auditors. Can the charge feeds go first when you come back up?"</span>`,`She wasn't wrong about the money. Glenn Morrow, the CFO, would care about year end too.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Let the charge feeds go first, since they're time bound and clinical feeds can wait.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`Charges drain first. ADT and results wait behind them for twenty minutes, and a Cheyenne nurse admits a patient into a room the lab thinks is still empty.`,
          why:`Patient care comes first, every time. Clinical feeds like ADT, orders, results, and pharmacy drain before financial ones. Financial deadlines are real, and you meet them by planning, not by making care wait.`},
        {t:`Drain ADT, orders, results, and pharmacy first, then charges, and reconcile.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The window runs 1 to 3 AM. ADT, orders, results, and pharmacy drain first, then charges, which finish by 3:40. Revenue cycle runs a count against the lab and pharmacy systems the next morning. Every charge is there once. The director sends Maria a note: <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Easiest year end I've had."</span>`,
          why:`Know your application families and what each one carries. Clinical systems come first after a downtime. Financial systems come next, with a reconciliation so nothing is lost or doubled. Planning the order ahead lets you protect both.`},
        {t:`Drain every feed at once at full speed, so all of it finishes as soon as possible.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Two hundred feeds hit MedaLink at once. It slows to a crawl and starts rejecting messages, and the drain takes twice as long as the plan.`,
          why:`Receiving systems can only file so fast. Draining everything at once floods them and can make the backlog worse. Sequence and throttle.`},
        {t:`Run the charge feeds alongside the clinical ones, each one throttled to half speed.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It works, mostly. Results take twice as long to clear as they needed to, and the charges are no faster.`,
          why:`Throttling is smart. Treating clinical and financial feeds as equals isn't. Set priority by what reaches patients, then throttle within it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s12`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L22`, title:`The binder`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Marcus was taking a week off after New Year's, his first in three years. His daughter was having a baby in Tucson. He came to Maria's office the afternoon of December 30 with a list of things that could go wrong while he was gone. It ran two pages, in pencil.`,
        ((s.flags.blamedmarcus||G.blamedmarcus)
          ?`He set it on her desk and didn't sit down. Things had been cool between them since the readout in Laramie. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus" data-tone="Cool, clipped, bitter.">"In case anybody needs someone to point at while I'm gone."</span>`
          :`He set it on her desk and sat down without being asked, which was new. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus" data-tone="Gruff, grudging, almost warm.">"You stood up for the design in Laramie. Figured I owed you a list."</span>`),
        `Maria had two junior analysts who'd asked to learn Relay. She'd asked Marcus twice. He'd changed the subject twice. This time she asked why.`,`He was quiet a long time. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus" data-tone="Low, slow, old bitterness.">"2009. Hospital in Omaha. They asked me to write everything down. I did. Good binder. Three months later they handed the binder to a contractor and handed me a box."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Make documentation part of his formal goals, so it gets done, and he knows it matters.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Marcus nods. He writes exactly what the goal asks for, and nothing that would make him easy to replace.`,
          why:`You just confirmed his fear. Writing it down was what got him a box last time. Competency spreads when the expert is safer teaching than hoarding.`},
        {t:`Send the two analysts to the Relay vendor's training course in the spring.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The course teaches the product. It doesn't teach Cascade's 214 interfaces, the firewall in Cheyenne, or why the dietary feed existed.`,
          why:`Formal training builds base skill, and it's worth doing. But the knowledge that matters lives in this system and in Marcus. Pair the training with time beside him.`},
        {t:`Make Marcus the teacher, in writing: paired runbooks, two trainees, and skill sign-offs.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She writes it into his role that afternoon: lead interface engineer, responsible for building the team's competency. The analysts will watch him, then work while he watches, then work alone, with a sign-off for each skill. He'll write the runbooks with them, not for them. Marcus reads it, then reads the last line again. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus" data-tone="Gruff, guarded, half pleased.">"This in my file?"</span> She says it is. He folds it into his vest pocket.`,
          why:`Staff competency comes from defined skills, practice, and verification, and from an expert who's rewarded for passing it on. Make the role explicit and protect the person. Then a single point of failure becomes a team.`},
        {t:`Hire a contractor to document Relay while he's away, so it's ready when he's back.`, s:0, d:{trust:-6,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Marcus finds out on the second day of his vacation, from a junior analyst's text. It's Omaha all over again, and he doesn't answer his phone the rest of the week.`,
          why:`Documentation without the expert is guesswork, and doing it behind his back costs you the person who knows the system. Build competency with him, not around him.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f3s2`, title:`How long the backlog takes`, setup:[`Marcus wants a number in the runbook so the next house supervisor knows how long late results will keep arriving. Laramie's lab sends about 12 results a minute at night. Suppose a night outage lasts 3 hours, and arrivals keep coming the whole time.`,`Messages queued = arrival rate times minutes down. Backlog clearance time = queued messages / (processing rate minus arrival rate). New results keep arriving while the queue drains, so only the difference shrinks it.`],
      fields:[{label:`Messages queued after 3 hours`, answer:2160, tol:0},{label:`Minutes to clear if Relay delivers 72 a minute`, answer:36, tol:0},{label:`Minutes to clear if MedaLink can only file 48 a minute`, answer:60, tol:0},{label:`Delivery rate needed to clear it in 20 minutes, per minute`, answer:120, tol:0}],
      solution:[`Queued: 12 a minute times 180 minutes = 2,160 messages.`,`At 72 a minute: 2,160 / (72 minus 12) = 2,160 / 60 = 36 minutes.`,`If MedaLink can only file 48 a minute, that's the real limit: 2,160 / (48 minus 12) = 2,160 / 36 = 60 minutes. The slowest system in the chain sets the pace.`,`To clear in 20 minutes the net drain has to be 2,160 / 20 = 108 a minute. Add back the 12 still arriving: 108 + 12 = 120 a minute. If the receiving system can't take that, the honest answer for the runbook is 60 minutes, and the house supervisor plans for it.`] },
    { after:`f3s5`, title:`Interface error rates`, setup:[`Relay's weekly report for three Wyoming interfaces. Laramie lab results: 41,600 messages, 208 rejected. Rawlins ADT: 18,400 messages, 1,104 rejected. Cheyenne orders: 52,000 messages, 780 rejected.`,`Interface error rate = rejected messages / total messages, as a percent. Round to two decimals.`],
      fields:[{label:`Laramie lab results error rate, percent`, answer:0.5, tol:0.01},{label:`Rawlins ADT error rate, percent`, answer:6, tol:0.01},{label:`Cheyenne orders error rate, percent`, answer:1.5, tol:0.01},{label:`Combined error rate for all three, percent`, answer:1.87, tol:0.01}],
      solution:[`Laramie: 208 / 41,600 = 0.005, or 0.50 percent.`,`Rawlins: 1,104 / 18,400 = 0.06, or 6.00 percent.`,`Cheyenne: 780 / 52,000 = 0.015, or 1.50 percent.`,`Combined: add the counts first, never the percents. (208 + 1,104 + 780) / (41,600 + 18,400 + 52,000) = 2,092 / 112,000 = 0.01868, or 1.87 percent.`,`Rawlins has the fewest messages and the worst rate. An ADT feed that rejects six in a hundred means patients whose location or identity didn't update somewhere downstream. That's the one to look at first, and it's a trend you'd only see by watching the rate, not the volume.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f3s7`, title:`Which standard?`, intro:`Every interface carries a standard, and the exam expects you to know which one fits the job. Pick the one that fits.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Registration tells the lab, pharmacy, and billing systems that a patient was just admitted to room 214.`, choices:[`DICOM`,`HL7 v2 ADT`,`X12 837`,`NCPDP SCRIPT`], answer:1, why:`ADT messages in HL7 version 2 carry admissions, transfers, discharges, and updates to every system that needs to know where a patient is.`},
      {prompt:`A CT scanner sends images to the PACS for a radiologist to read.`, choices:[`DICOM`,`FHIR`,`LOINC`,`C-CDA`], answer:0, why:`DICOM is the standard for medical images and the information that travels with them.`},
      {prompt:`The hospital sends a claim for an inpatient stay to a commercial insurer.`, choices:[`HL7 v2 ORU`,`NCPDP SCRIPT`,`X12 837`,`SNOMED CT`], answer:2, why:`X12 transactions carry business with payers. The 837 is the claim. The 835 is the payment and remittance advice.`},
      {prompt:`A physician sends a new prescription electronically to the patient's retail pharmacy.`, choices:[`X12 270`,`HL7 v2 ADT`,`DICOM`,`NCPDP SCRIPT`], answer:3, why:`NCPDP SCRIPT is the standard for electronic prescribing between prescribers and pharmacies.`},
      {prompt:`A summary of a patient's problems, medications, and allergies goes with the patient at a transfer.`, choices:[`C-CDA`,`X12 835`,`ICD-10-PCS`,`DICOM`], answer:0, why:`The Continuity of Care Document, part of C-CDA, is built for transitions of care.`},
      {prompt:`A patient's phone app pulls their lab results from the hospital through an API.`, choices:[`HL7 v2 ORM`,`FHIR`,`X12 837`,`CPT`], answer:1, why:`FHIR exchanges data as resources over web APIs. It's the standard behind patient app access.`},
      {prompt:`Two hospitals agree that a lactate result means the same test, wherever it was run.`, choices:[`RxNorm`,`ICD-10-CM`,`LOINC`,`CPT`], answer:2, why:`LOINC is the vocabulary for lab tests and clinical observations.`},
      {prompt:`A pharmacy system and an EHR agree on one normalized name for a drug and its strength.`, choices:[`SNOMED CT`,`LOINC`,`DICOM`,`RxNorm`], answer:3, why:`RxNorm gives clinical drugs normalized names, so different systems can agree on the same medication.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`An interface engine holds a long lived connection through a firewall. A firewall change shortens the idle timeout, the connection drops silently, and messages queue for hours. What is the BEST long-term fix?`, opts:[`Restore the old timeout and lock the firewall settings`,`Have an engineer check each connection every evening`,`Add ACK timeouts, reconnects, and dependency checks`,`Freeze firewall changes until the next EHR upgrade`], a:2, why:`Fix both layers: the engine should detect a dead connection and recover, and the change process should know which clinical systems depend on the network. Then test it by breaking it on purpose.`, task:`E7`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`What is the main advantage of an interface engine over point to point interfaces?`, opts:[`Each system connects once to a central hub`,`It removes the need for any message standards`,`It makes every application share one database`,`It eliminates the need for network monitoring`], a:0, why:`A hub reduces the number of connections, translates and routes messages, and centralizes logging and monitoring. It still needs standards, monitoring, and redundancy.`, task:`E7`, domain:`Environments`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`In a patient safety review, a manager concludes that the engineer who missed an alert caused the outage. Which response BEST supports a culture where staff keep reporting problems?`, opts:[`Agree, and handle it with the engineer in private`,`Move the discussion on to the firewall change`,`Name the system causes and own the leader's part`,`Call it a personnel matter and end the meeting`], a:2, why:`A just culture looks for the conditions that let a failure happen. Leaders who name system causes and own their part keep staff reporting; settling on the nearest person teaches everyone to stay quiet.`, task:`L2`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A data warehouse team asks for direct query access to the production EHR database to build a monthly turnaround report. What is the BEST response?`, opts:[`Grant read-only access after midnight only`,`Build it from the reporting copy instead`,`Ask the EHR vendor to run the report for you`,`Pull the times from interface engine logs only`], a:1, why:`Analytics belongs on a warehouse or reporting copy. Heavy queries on a production transaction system can slow clinical work, and nights are still patient care.`, task:`E6`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`After a planned interface engine downtime, several queues will drain at once. Which should drain FIRST?`, opts:[`ADT, orders, and results feeds`,`All feeds at full speed, at the same time`,`Charges, since they have a year end deadline`,`Feeds with the largest backlog`], a:0, why:`Clinical feeds that carry patient location, orders, and results come first, with ADT ahead of orders and results, because orders and results need the patient's location first. Financial feeds follow, with a reconciliation so nothing is lost or doubled.`, task:`E6`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`An alert for interface queue depth fired during an outage but went to one engineer's email, where a filter hid it. What does analysis of this failure point to FIRST?`, opts:[`The engineer's failure to read the alert`,`The alert's routing, thresholds, and duty`,`The need for a new monitoring platform`,`The firewall change that began the outage`], a:1, why:`Analyze the whole signal chain: what's measured, the threshold, who gets it at what hour, and what they're supposed to do. One person missing one alert among thousands is a design failure.`, task:`SC6`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Which data source is MOST likely to show an interface problem before anyone recognizes it as an interface failure?`, opts:[`The annual user satisfaction survey`,`The vendor's monthly release notes`,`Help desk tickets about slow results`,`The interface engine's license report`], a:2, why:`Help desk logs often show the symptom first, like nurses reporting slow results. Linking tickets to interface status turns them into early warning.`, task:`SC6`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Bedside monitor vitals arrive in the EHR seven minutes off because the device gateway's time source was retired. What is the BEST fix?`, opts:[`Offset every vitals message in the interface`,`Have nurses chart vitals by hand for now`,`Ask the device vendor to reset the clock`,`Fix the time source with biomed, then validate`], a:3, why:`Fix the source. Clinical engineering owns the devices, IT owns the network and time service, and a validation test proves the times are right before clinicians rely on them.`, task:`SB1`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Smart infusion pumps that receive the ordered rate from the EHR and send back what was given are an example of:`, opts:[`Medical device integration`,`Clinical decision support`,`Business intelligence`,`Health information exchange`], a:0, why:`Moving data between bedside devices and the EHR is medical device integration. It reduces manual programming and documentation errors.`, task:`SB1`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Three hospitals send lab results with local codes, and leaders want one report across all of them. What is the BEST approach?`, opts:[`Build a code table inside the report itself`,`Wait for the new EHR to standardize codes`,`Have one analyst map all codes, unreviewed`,`Map to LOINC, with the lab owning the map`], a:3, why:`LOINC is the standard for lab tests. The lab owns what each test means, IT builds the mapping at the interface, and validation proves it before anyone uses the data.`, task:`SB2`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A system sends a lab result to a hospital and the hospital receives it, but the result's local code can't be understood by the receiving system. Which level of interoperability is missing?`, opts:[`Foundational`,`Structural`,`Semantic`,`Organizational`], a:2, why:`The data arrived (foundational) in an agreed format (structural), but the two sides don't share meaning. That's semantic interoperability, usually solved with standard vocabularies.`, task:`SB2`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`In an HL7 v2 message, which segment carries an individual lab result value?`, opts:[`MSH`,`PID`,`PV1`,`OBX`], a:3, why:`MSH is the message header, PID identifies the patient, PV1 describes the visit, and OBX carries each observation or result value.`, task:`C2`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Delayed lab results arrive for patients who were discharged during an outage, and the EHR rejects them because the visits are closed. What should happen?`, opts:[`File each result to the patient's newest visit`,`Match each result to an open chart by name and DOB`,`Reopen the visits in bulk and file every result`,`File each result to its visit and tell each physician`], a:3, why:`The account number ties a result to its encounter, and the master patient index ties the record to the person. File each result where it belongs, then close the loop with the ordering or discharging clinician.`, task:`C2`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`One interface engineer is the only person who understands the interface engine. What is the BEST way to reduce this risk?`, opts:[`Hire a contractor to document it without him`,`Make him the teacher, with skill sign-offs`,`Make documentation one of his performance goals`,`Send other analysts to the vendor's training`], a:1, why:`Staff competency comes from defined skills, supervised practice, and verification, led by the expert in a role that rewards teaching. Vendor training helps but can't replace local knowledge.`, task:`L22`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A firewall vendor that will receive copies of logs containing patient names to troubleshoot an outage must FIRST sign a:`, opts:[`Master services agreement`,`Service level agreement`,`Business associate agreement`,`Mutual nondisclosure agreement`], a:2, why:`A vendor that receives PHI on a covered entity's behalf is a business associate and must sign a business associate agreement before it gets the data.`, task:`E4`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A clinic reaches the hospital's systems across the public internet. Which technology builds a secure tunnel for that traffic?`, opts:[`A local area network`,`A virtual private network`,`An interface engine hub`,`A wireless access point`], a:1, why:`A VPN builds an encrypted tunnel across a network you don't control, like the internet. A local area network connects devices inside a building, an interface engine routes messages between applications, and a wireless access point connects devices over the air.`, task:`E7`, domain:`Environments`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A patient on a medical unit is moved to the ICU. Which HL7 v2 message tells the lab, pharmacy, and dietary systems about the move?`, opts:[`ADT A01`,`ADT A02`,`ADT A03`,`ADT A08`], a:1, why:`ADT messages tell every connected system where the patient is. A01 is an admission, A02 a transfer, A03 a discharge, and A08 an update to patient information. A move from one unit to another is a transfer.`, task:`C2`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A discharge forecasting tool in the business intelligence layer suddenly predicts far fewer discharges. Nothing in the tool itself changed. Which input should the team check FIRST?`, opts:[`The ADT and census feed it reads`,`The pharmacy dispensing cabinet feed`,`The billing system's charge feed`,`The portal's patient message feed`], a:0, why:`Business intelligence tools depend on the feeds that load them. A discharge forecast is built on admissions, transfers, discharges, and census, so a broken or changed ADT feed is the likeliest cause. Pharmacy, billing, and portal feeds belong to other application families and don't drive a discharge prediction.`, task:`E6`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AN`},
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `The patient safety review closed on the Tuesday between Christmas and New Year's. Four causes, four owners, eleven actions, and every one of them with a date. Ochoa read the final report at their one-on-one and set it down without a question, which Maria was learning was his highest compliment.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Quiet, approving, measured.">"Two months ago you'd have fixed the interface,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Quiet, approving, measured.">"This time you fixed the building it lives in. Keep doing that. It's slower and it's the job."</span>`,
    ((s.flags.blamedmarcus||G.blamedmarcus)
      ?`Marcus's list sat on her desk, two pages in pencil. He'd left it and gone without saying goodbye. She'd said one thing in a Laramie conference room that she couldn't unsay, and he'd remember it longer than any fix she made.`
      :`Marcus flew to Tucson on New Year's Day. That night he sent her a photo of the whiteboard in the basement, all of Relay's boxes and arrows, and nothing else.`),
    `Lucia was on the couch when she got home, a practice question for her nursing school interview open on her phone. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"What's a med rec?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Medication reconciliation. When a patient moves, somebody checks the list they came with against the list they're supposed to be on."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Offhand, bored, teasing.">"Sounds boring."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Dry, light.">"It is,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, far away, heavy.">"Until the list is short."</span> Lucia looked up at her, waiting for the rest. Maria went to start dinner instead.`,
    (s.score>=30
      ?`It had been a long two months, and most of it had gone right. The ceiling was dry. She knew where the pipes ran now, and who knew them, and that the answer to who knew them couldn't be one man in a fleece vest. She had a plan for that.`
      :`It had been a long two months, and she'd want some of it back. The ceiling was dry, mostly. She knew where the pipes ran now. She also knew how many of her own choices had made the next leak more likely, and Ochoa would make sure she remembered them.`),
    `Two emails came in on Monday, January 4, three minutes apart.`,
    `The first was from Aisha Rahman. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha" data-tone="Urgent, intense, determined.">"The lactate baseline is ugly. Sepsis is killing people in Wyoming slower than it should, but it's killing them. I want a sepsis alert live in all eleven hospitals by spring. I'm bringing it to the council next week. I'd like you to co-sponsor."</span>`,
    `The second was from the Laramie medical staff office. It had an attachment.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Formal complaint, medical staff office">Submitted by Walter Hagen, MD, General Surgery, Laramie. Subject: click counts. Since changes made in December after the lab interface outage, a routine discharge now takes forty-seven clicks in MedaLink, up from forty-one. I was told the new system would be better. I would like it noted that the old one just got worse.</prop>`,
    `Maria sat with them both until her coffee went cold. Then she opened her calendar and blocked a week in Wyoming, on the floor, in scrubs.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch03.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Floor`
});
